course: english
lesson: 2
title: Blending, segmenting and the Year 1 check
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Reading and spelling are the same skill going in two directions. Reading is blending: you see «stop», say the sounds /s/ /t/ /o/ /p/ and push them together into the word. Spelling is segmenting: you hear «frog», tap one finger for each sound, f-r-o-g, and write a letter or a letter team for each tap.
explain: The sounds must be short and clipped. Noah says «suh-tuh-o-puh» and gets «sutop», a word that does not exist, because every «uh» is an extra sound that is not in the word. Said cleanly, s-t-o-p slides into «stop».
explain: Long words are read in chunks called syllables, the beats you can clap: sand-wich, pic-nic, fan-tas-tic. You blend each chunk, then glue the chunks together. A slow reader who sounds out «the dog ran» one sound at a time is at a normal stage; re-reading easy books makes words instant.
explain: In England, Year 1 children (about six years old) do the phonics screening check: they read 40 words to a teacher, half real and half made-up. The made-up words catch guessing: Ethan reads «chom» as «chomp» because it looks like a word he knows. It is a check to find who needs help, not a pass or fail for life; anyone who misses it gets help and takes it again in Year 2.
explain: Reading the words aloud is not the same as understanding them. A boy can read every word of a story and still not know what happened, so grown-ups ask «Who was in it? What happened?» after each page. And mistakes like reading «shop» as «sop» are normal: the child read the s and missed its partner h.
fact: Blending is pushing sounds together to read a word; segmenting is splitting a word into its sounds to spell it.
fact: Pure, clipped sounds (/s/, not «suh») blend cleanly; adding «uh» puts extra sounds into the word.
fact: Sound buttons are dots under the letters, one for each sound; a letter team such as sh gets one button.
fact: A syllable is a beat in a word with one vowel sound; long words are read chunk by chunk, sand-wich.
fact: In England, the Year 1 phonics screening check asks a child to read 40 words to a teacher, some real and some made-up.
fact: A child who does not reach the expected mark gets extra help and takes the check again in Year 2.
fact: Accurate decoding means reading every sound in order; guessing means saying a word that just looks similar.
fact: Slow sounding-out is a normal early stage; re-reading the same easy book builds speed until words are known at a glance.
fact: Decoding (turning letters into words) and comprehension (understanding what you read) are two different skills.
fact: A decodable book uses only the sounds a child has already been taught.
fact: Some common words such as «was» have a tricky bit (the a says /o/, the s says /z/); the rest still follows the rules.
fact: Praise for every answer, right or wrong, teaches nothing; good feedback says what was right and gently fixes what was wrong.
fact: Slang in this lesson: «mardy» means sulky or grumpy (North and Midlands of England); «yinz» means «you all» in Pittsburgh; «craic» is Irish for fun; «eejit» is Irish for idiot.
exercise: Noah reads «suh-tuh-o-puh» and says «sutop». What went wrong? | He added an «uh» to each sound | He read the letters from right to left | The word has a silent letter he missed | He said the letter names instead of sounds
exercise: Zara taps a finger for each sound in «frog». What is she doing? | Segmenting the word to spell it | Blending the word to read it aloud | Counting the letters of the alphabet in order | Clapping the beats of a long word
exercise: How many sounds does Zara tap for «frog»? | 4 | 3 | 5 | 2
exercise: A Leeds mum calls the check «proper mardy of 'em». What does «mardy» mean? | Sulky or grumpy | Clever and kind | Very fast | Old-fashioned and silly
exercise: How many words does a child read in the Year 1 phonics screening check? | 40 | 10 | 100 | 400
exercise: What happens to a child who does not reach the mark in Year 1? | They get help and take the check again in Year 2 | They have to repeat the whole of Year 1 again | They are not allowed to read books for a year | Nothing, and nobody ever checks their reading again
exercise: Ethan reads the made-up word «chom» as «chomp». What is he doing? | Guessing from a word he knows | Reading every sound carefully | Blending pure, clipped sounds | Using the tricky-word list
exercise: Isla can read «cat» but gets stuck on «sandwich». What helps? | Read it in chunks: sand, wich | Learn the whole word as one picture | Read it backwards, letter by letter | Skip the long words in every story
exercise: A Pittsburgh grandpa says «yinz kids read so slow». What does «yinz» mean? | You all | Little ones | Lazy | Young
exercise: Lily reads «the dog ran» one sound at a time. What does that show? | A normal early stage that gets faster | She will always be a slow reader | Her book is far too easy for her and should be swapped | She has been taught the wrong sounds
exercise: Jack's app shouts «Great job!» even when he reads a word wrong. What is the problem? | It gives no real feedback on his reading | It is far too loud for a small child to hear properly | It praises him too rarely | It only works in American English
exercise: A boy reads every word aloud but can't say what happened in the story. What is missing? | Comprehension, understanding what he read | Decoding, turning letters into sounds | Handwriting, writing the words down neatly on the line | Blending, gluing sounds together
exercise: Priya reads «was» as «wass». Which part of «was» is tricky? | The a and the s, which say /o/ and /z/ | The w, which is silent in this word | All three letters, none follows the rules | Nothing; «wass» is the right way to say it
exercise: Dad in Cork laughs because his girl read «shop» as «sop». What did she miss? | The h that teams up with s to say /sh/ | The o, which she should have said as /oa/ | The p at the end, which she left out | A silent e at the very end of the word
exercise: What is a decodable book? | A book using only sounds already taught | A book with no words, only pictures | A secret book written in a code | A book for grown-ups who read fast and silently in their heads
exercise: What is a syllable? | A beat in a word with one vowel sound | A single letter of the alphabet | Two letters that make one sound | A word that has no vowels in it at all, like «rhythm» or «gym»
test: «ma lad reads all the words but he couldn't tell ya what happened, like». What should his mum do? | Ask one easy question after each page and talk about the story | Nothing, reading the words aloud means he understood them | Make him read faster so the story sticks | Stop the stories for now and do only sound drills every single night
test: «yinz kids are slow, back in my day we just read 'er!» What is true about slow early readers? | Slow sounding-out is normal and speeds up with re-reading easy books | Slow early readers will stay slow for their whole lives | Fast reading in the first week shows a child is clever | Grandpa's way, reading without any sounds at all, is quicker for every child everywhere
test: «a test at six?? they'll fail her and she's stuck forever». Calm this Leeds mum down. | It finds who needs help, and anyone who misses it tries again in Year 2 | She's right, missing the check means staying behind for ever | The check is only for children who already read well | Nobody is told the result, so it doesn't matter at all
test: «ahh the craic, she read shop as sop, is she an eejit or what». What is the wise answer? | It's a normal mistake: show her that s and h are a team | Yes, a child who misses letters is not clever | Make her read the word fifty times until it sticks | Tell her «shop» is really spelled with one letter less than she thinks
test: What would happen if the check had only real words? | Children could pass by remembering words instead of reading them | Every child would fail, because real words are too hard | Nothing at all would change in what the check shows | The check would take twice as long for each child
test: «just read me the story, why do I hafta tap fingers to spell frog». Why tap? | Each tap is one sound, so you know how many to write | Tapping makes the hand warm and ready to write | It's a game with no link to spelling at all | Tapping counts the letters, and frog has five
test: Ethan said «chomp» for «chom». His mum says «well done, nearly». What should she do? | Point under each letter and ask what it says | Praise the guess, because fast is what counts | Tell him made-up words don't matter | Show him a picture of someone chomping
test: A cartoon robot reads «sandwich» by shouting all eight sounds at once. What do real readers do? | Read it chunk by chunk, sand then wich, then join | Learn the whole word as a shape, like a picture | Shout every letter's name in turn | Give up and skip all the long words, because only short words can be sounded out
test: Mum: «the app said great job so he must be reading great». Is she right? | No, the app praises everything, so it shows nothing | Yes, apps always check reading better than people | Yes, any reading app is as good as a teacher | No, apps make children forget how to read
test: ★★★ How many sounds are there in «chips»? Careful! | 4: ch, i, p, s | 5: c, h, i, p, s | 3: chi, p, s | 6: c, h, i, p, s and a silent e
test: ★★★ Noah adds «uh» to every sound in «crisp». How many extra sounds does he add? | 4, one after each consonant | 5, one after every letter | 1, only at the end | none, because «uh» is not a sound
test: ★★★ A made-up word has sound buttons: one dot under th, one under i, one under ng. How many sounds and letters? | 3 sounds, 5 letters | 5 sounds, 5 letters | 3 sounds, 3 letters | 5 sounds, 3 letters
exam: Select the response which most accurately distinguishes blending from segmenting. | Blending combines sounds to read a word, whereas segmenting separates a word into sounds in order to spell it | Blending and segmenting are two names for an identical process, employed in reading and in spelling alike, without distinction | Segmenting combines sounds to read, whereas blending separates them | Blending pertains to handwriting, whereas segmenting pertains to punctuation
exam: It is given that a pupil in Year 1 does not attain the expected standard in the phonics screening check. Ascertain the consequence. | The pupil receives further support and undertakes the check again in Year 2 | The pupil is permanently excluded from instruction in reading | The pupil must repeat the entire year without exception | No further action is taken in respect of the pupil
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the inclusion of pseudo-words in the said check. | To detect pupils who guess at familiar words rather than decode | To amuse the pupils during an otherwise tedious examination | To teach the pupils new vocabulary for later use | To shorten the time required for the check
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relation between decoding and comprehension? | They are distinct skills; a pupil may decode a text without comprehending it | Comprehension invariably follows from accurate decoding | Decoding is impossible without prior comprehension of the text | They are a single skill bearing two names
